<SiteLayout title="GZIP vs ZIP: What's the Difference Between .gz and .zip? | ConvertAnyFile" description="Learn why GZIP usually compresses one stream while ZIP stores multiple files, how TAR.GZ fits in, and when converting GZ to ZIP is useful.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>GZIP vs ZIP</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Archive guide</span><h1>GZIP vs ZIP: why .gz is not the same as .zip</h1><p>The names sound similar, but GZIP and ZIP solve different packaging problems. GZIP compresses a byte stream, while ZIP is an archive container that can hold many files and directories.</p></div></section>
<ArticleFrame toc={toc} related={related} relatedTitle="Related archive tools">
<div class="quick-answer" id="quick"><strong>Quick answer</strong><p>A normal <code>.gz</code> file is a gzip-compressed single stream. A <code>.zip</code> file is a multi-entry archive. If you need a regular archive around the file recovered from GZIP, use the <a href="/gz-to-zip/">GZIP to ZIP converter</a>.</p></div>
<h2 id="difference">GZIP vs ZIP</h2><div class="comparison-table-wrap"><table class="comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>GZIP</th><th>ZIP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary role</td><td>Compress one stream</td><td>Archive files and directories</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple named entries</td><td>No, not by itself</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Common extension</td><td>.gz / .gzip</td><td>.zip</td></tr><tr><td>Often paired with TAR</td><td>Yes</td><td>No requirement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="single">Why does GZIP usually contain one file?</h2><p>GZIP does not provide a general directory tree like ZIP. It compresses an input stream and can record limited information such as an original filename. To compress a directory tree with gzip, Unix workflows commonly create a TAR archive first and then gzip the TAR stream.</p>
<h2 id="convert">When should you convert GZIP to ZIP?</h2><p>Convert GZIP to ZIP when you have a single <code>.gz</code> payload but the destination expects a ZIP archive. The browser decompresses the GZIP stream, recovers a safe filename from the source name, and places that file into a new ZIP container.</p><p>This does not make the inner file a different format. For example, a compressed text file remains text after it is moved from a GZIP stream into ZIP.</p>
<h2 id="tar">Where does TAR.GZ fit?</h2><p>TAR.GZ is different from generic GZIP because the decompressed payload is itself a TAR archive. A TAR.GZ or TGZ can therefore contain a directory tree and many files. If your source is TAR.GZ and the goal is ZIP, use the dedicated <a href="/tar-gz-to-zip/">TAR.GZ to ZIP converter</a> so both layers are handled correctly.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Filename matters</strong><p><code>backup.tar.gz</code> is a compressed TAR archive; <code>notes.txt.gz</code> is normally a compressed single file. They should not be treated as the same conversion workflow.</p></div>
